<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luca Giordano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1634–<a href="/years/1705">1705</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luca Giordano (1634–1705) was a Neapolitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> painter, nicknamed Fa Presto for the speed of his work. Influenced by Ribera and the Venetians, he painted large altarpieces and fresco cycles across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, including the ceiling of the Palazzo Medici Riccardi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, and worked for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain">Charles II of Spain</a> from 1692 to 1702, decorating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-escorial">El Escorial</a>. He was the leading painter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in his time.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luca-giordano.jpg" alt="Luca Giordano" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luca Giordano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luca_Giordano_Fapresto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
